// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// specs/t27os/group_core.t27 -- the core of group chat in T27 OS (3 to 8 people): pure decisions, no @extern, no
// crypto. A group message travels as one more kind of payload inside the person to person message session of each
// member (chat_core.t27, call_session.t27): no new transport. The disk and the session driving are chat_host.t27.
// Owner, 2026-10-23. Refs gHashTag/t27#11560. ASCII only (L3), English only (LANG-EN).
//
// GROUP  2..GR_MEMBERS (8) people. Its id is the first 8 bytes of SHA-256 over the members' fingerprints sorted
//   ascending and joined (gr_roster gives those n * 10 bytes, the host hashes, gr_id_from_digest cuts): the same
//   people always give the same id, whatever order they were picked in. The creator picks only friends of its own
//   friends table (gr_plan); someone else's group is kept only when its creator is a friend and this device is in it
//   (gr_known).
// TABLE  ~/.t27os/groups.t27, v1, the bytes in memory are the file's first GT_HDR + count * GR_REC bytes:
//   magic "T27OSGR\n" (8) | version u32 | count u32 (<= GT_MAX, 32) | count records of GR_REC (132) bytes: id (8) |
//   time u64 (ms since 1970, made) | members n u8 | creator index u8 | name length u8 | 0 | name (32, UTF-8, no
//   control byte, zero after its length) | n fingerprints of 10, sorted ascending, zero after them (80) | CRC-32
//   (peer_identity.crc32) of all before (4). Written whole and atomically by the host, mode 0600.
// FRAME  a payload of a sealed CK_T_TEXT frame, GM_HDR (38) bytes then the bytes of one part:
//   kind u8 (GM_K_TEXT 4, GM_K_ACK 5; chat_core's are 1..3) | 0 | group id (8) | sender fingerprint (10) | msg u32 LE
//   (1, 2, .. per sender and group, below 2^24) | part u16 | parts u16 | time u64 (the sender's ms) | len u16 | len
//   bytes. A message is 1..2000 bytes of UTF-8 in parts of 700 (chat_core's limits), so a payload is at most 738
//   bytes and a sealed frame 52 + 738 + 16 = 806. An ACK names the message's author in the sender field: len 0,
//   parts 0, time 0.
// DELIVERY  fan-out: the author sends every part to each member, over the message session with that member. Who
//   is a recipient is fixed when the line is made (gr_fanout): a member that is a friend is sent to now when
//   reachable, queued otherwise; a member that is not a friend of the author cannot be reached (no session) and is
//   skipped. The line is DELIVERED once every recipient acknowledged every part. A receiver keeps a GROUP payload
//   only when the group is in its table, it is in the group, the sender is a member, the sender is the proven peer
//   of the session it came over (no relaying), and that peer's key is stored (gr_gate); else it is dropped with the
//   reason, and not acknowledged.
// LOG  a chat_core log (same layout in memory, same file) whose "peer" is the group id and two zero bytes. Its file
//   is ~/.t27os/chat/g-<16 hex of the id>.t27. An incoming line's number is the sender's member index (sorted
//   order) times 2^24 plus the sender's number, so chat_core's dedup by (direction, number) is dedup by (sender,
//   number); an outgoing line keeps this device's own number. Lines stand in the order of (time, sender
//   fingerprint, number): equal times never swap. The log's single floor is kept at 0; the floor per sender sits in
//   an 8 x u32 block of the driver (gl_floors_*). The 5 spare bytes of a line (chat_core CL_EXT) hold, for an
//   outgoing line: the members already sent to in this round (1) | ACK bits, 3 per member index (3) | recipients (1).
//   Unread is per group: chat_core cl_unread of the group's log.
module T27osGroupCore;

